---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CaseHeader from '../../components/CaseHeader.astro';

const layers = [
  {
    title: 'Skill library',
    body: 'Diagnostic know-how lives in plain Markdown skill files: what to check, in what order, and what the result means. The skills are the asset; the model is replaceable infrastructure.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Tiered model routing',
    body: 'Start every step on the smallest language model that can handle it, and escalate to a larger model only when a step needs more reasoning. The goal is predictable cost and latency without giving up hard cases.',
  },
  {
    title: 'PowerShell action layer',
    body: 'The agent acts through PowerShell tools with structured JSON in and out under a published contract. The model never reads raw logs; it reads the tool’s summary.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Audit trail',
    body: 'Agent actions are written to a JSONL audit log, so a technician or reviewer can see what the agent did.',
  },
];

const scenarios = ['BitLocker recovery', 'Slow boot with a SMART regression', 'Blue screen after an update'];

const notShown = [
  'Field use on live repair tickets.',
  'Measured cost, speed, or accuracy. There are no validated numbers yet, so this page quotes none.',
  'A public repository or demo.',
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Anvil: tiered agentic diagnostics" description="Anvil is a paused prototype of a bench-repair assistant: diagnostic skill files, small language models first, and PowerShell tools behind a structured JSON contract.">
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16">
    <CaseHeader
      title="Anvil"
      subtitle="An agentic diagnostics assistant for repair benches: small language models driving PowerShell tools, organised in tiers."
      status="Paused prototype"
      statusTone="amber"
      statusLine="Status as of October 4, 2026: paused prototype. Architecture and an early Python CLI exist; no field testing, no public repo."
    />

    <div class="space-y-12 fade-in">
      <section>
        <h2 class="font-mono text-xl font-semibold text-text-secondary mb-4">The problem</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-text-secondary/85 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            On a repair bench, the difference between a fast fix and a lost afternoon is usually what the most
            experienced tech knows to check first. That knowledge lives in people's heads, so it doesn't scale and
            it walks out the door.
          </p>
          <p>
            Anvil is my attempt to package that know-how so a small AI agent can retrieve the right procedure, run
            the right checks through scripted tools, and walk a technician through the fix, while staying cheap,
            inspectable, and under the technician's control.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-mono text-xl font-semibold text-text-secondary mb-4">Architecture</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          {layers.map((l, i) => (
            <div class="border border-border-color rounded-lg p-5">
              <h3 class="font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-terminal-green mb-2">{i + 1}. {l.title}</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary/75 leading-relaxed">{l.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-mono text-xl font-semibold text-text-secondary mb-4">Design scenarios</h2>
        <p class="text-text-secondary/85 leading-relaxed mb-4">The early prototype was shaped around common Windows bench cases:</p>
        <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          {scenarios.map((s) => <li class="font-mono text-xs text-text-secondary/85 bg-terminal-green/5 border border-border-color px-3 py-1.5 rounded">{s}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-mono text-xl font-semibold text-text-secondary mb-4">Why it matters</h2>
        <p class="text-text-secondary/85 leading-relaxed">
          Most enterprise agent projects face the same trade-offs: cost per task, whether you can swap models,
          what the agent is allowed to touch, and whether anyone can audit what it did. Anvil's answers are small
          models first, knowledge kept outside the model, tools with a narrow contract, and a log of every action.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="border border-alert-amber/40 rounded-lg p-6 bg-alert-amber/5">
        <h2 class="font-mono text-base font-semibold text-alert-amber mb-3">What this does not show</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-text-secondary/80">
          {notShown.map((n) => <li>{n}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-mono text-xl font-semibold text-text-secondary mb-4">Stack</h2>
        <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          {['Python', 'PowerShell', 'Small language models', 'Tiered routing', 'BM25 retrieval', 'JSONL audit log'].map((t) => (
            <li class="font-mono text-xs text-text-secondary/85 bg-terminal-green/5 border border-border-color px-3 py-1 rounded">{t}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
